#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110600 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110343 :question/id #uuid "b5470454-6a27-5eda-a323-74ce90259f4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14734" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $4x - 2y = 10?$\n\n- $y = \\dfrac{1}{2}x + 3$\n\n- $y = -4x + 1$\n\n- $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $4x - 2y = 10,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =10 \\\\ -2y & =-4x+10 \\\\ y & =2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $2.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$ has a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$ is perpendicular to the given line.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049201 :topic/math-academy-id 3725 :topic/title "Properties of Lines Given in Standard Form"}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514750 :question-generator/id #uuid "7a61d7f9-d3f9-5cfe-a98f-2bbfe440e024" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186110600.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04aa8a38-9ecc-5e87-9899-fa2c52620615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326982} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0d4423-cdbd-59bc-9519-c02229ca242c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327296} {:answer/id #uuid "842c8b78-fb24-5dfb-9556-59e